<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    Type campaign, item `charptr`: a pointer the program only ever uses on
    characters is declared as an integer or as `void *`, although the fact is
    already on the table.  coreutils `realpath` -O0 shows it, on
    `path_prefix(char const *prefix, char const *path)`:

        bool sub_2bbc(void *a0,void *a1)

    and the body walks both arguments one byte at a time.

    Root cause (P5, type propagation).  `TypeOpCall::getInputLocal`
    (typeop.cc:689) states a callee's declared parameter type about the Varnode
    that IS the call's operand, and `propagateTypeEdge` moves a Varnode only to
    a MORE specific type.  Every hop between the value a reader sees declared
    and the use that says "characters" - an -O0 spill through the frame, a
    MULTIEQUAL, a `p + k` whose INT_ADD refuses to carry a pointer back
    (typeop.cc:1217, the same refusal `ptrfromuse` documents) - is a hop the
    fact does not cross.

    Stage / sub-stage: P5 "type-propagation".  The P0 assertion
    `option charptr on|off` decides whether character evidence may commit a
    pointer-width parameter or stack local to `char *`.

    The fixture is the same shape on SysV x86-64, plus four controls:

        spillcall:  add rdi,1; call slen    where `char *slen(char *)`
                    the witness - the declaration is one INT_ADD away from the
                    parameter, which is the hop propagation cannot cross
        scaled:     shl rdi,2; call slen
                    a control - RDI is SHIFTED before it reaches the declared
                    `char *`, which no pointer survives
        fieldbyte:  movzx eax, byte [rdi+0x3c]
                    a control - a one-byte read at a FIXED offset is a struct
                    field, not a character.  coreutils `ginstall`'s
                    `announce_mkdir(char *dir, void *options)` reads the `bool`
                    at `options + 0x3c`, and typing that `char *` would replace
                    a correct `void *` with a wrong claim
        mixedwidth: movzx eax, byte [rdi]; add eax,[rdi+8]
                    a control - the same value is also read four bytes wide, so
                    the byte read does not establish the element type
        otherproto: add rdi,1; call ilen    where `int8 ilen(int8 *)`
                    a control - the callee declares a pointer at something else,
                    which contradicts a character pointer rather than
                    supporting one

    `prefixcmp` is the corpus witness written out in full (the byte-at-a-time
    compare `path_prefix` runs), and it is here as a THIRD control: kuna already
    types it `char *` without the option, through the PTRADD the
    pointer-arithmetic pool builds, and it must not move either way.

    Pass 1 (`option charptr off`) reproduces the bug; pass 2 (`on`) is the fix.
    All five controls are identical in both passes.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000">

</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="spillcall"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100020" name="scaled"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100040" name="prefixcmp"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000c0" name="fieldbyte"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000e0" name="mixedwidth"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100100" name="otherproto"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100140" name="slen"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100150" name="ilen"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>parse line extern char *slen(char *p);</com>
  <com>parse line extern int8 ilen(int8 *p);</com>
  <com>option charptr off</com>
  <com>lo fu spillcall</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu scaled</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu prefixcmp</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu fieldbyte</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu mixedwidth</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu otherproto</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option charptr on</com>
  <com>lo fu spillcall</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu scaled</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu prefixcmp</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu fieldbyte</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu mixedwidth</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu otherproto</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="charptr #1: with the option off a parameter a declared char * is one addition away from is an integer - the bug" min="1" max="1">void spillcall\(int8 a0\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="charptr #2: with the option on the callee's declaration reaches it" min="1" max="1">void spillcall\(char \*a0\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="charptr #3: a parameter shifted before it reaches the declaration is refused in both passes" min="2" max="2">void scaled\(int8 a0\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="charptr #4: a one-byte read at a fixed offset is a struct field, so the pointer keeps its placeholder in both passes" min="2" max="2">char fieldbyte\(void \*a0\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="charptr #5: a value also read four bytes wide is not a character pointer in either pass" min="2" max="2">int4 mixedwidth\(struct_0 \*a0\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="charptr #6: a callee that declares a pointer at something else contradicts a character pointer in both passes" min="2" max="2">void otherproto\(int8 a0\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="charptr #7: the byte-at-a-time compare is already char * without the option, and the option does not move it" min="2" max="2">char prefixcmp\(char \*a0,char \*a1\)</stringmatch>
